Modifications made for burtcraft:

  MODIFIED src/api/java/baritone/api/Settings.java
           - Added `avoidWaterWhileDry`: while the player is dry, entering water
             is restricted to the bounded crossing check described below.
           - Added `smallWaterCrossingMaxLength` (default 6): the longest run of
             water a dry path may cross. Ponds and rivers stay pathable; an
             ocean does not become a shortcut.
           - Added `swimCostMultiplier` (default 2): scales the cost of every
             swimming movement. Vanilla prices a swum block at roughly twice a
             walked one, which is why long ocean crossings used to win on cost.
           - Changed the default of the debug render settings (renderPath,
             renderPathAsLine, renderGoal, renderGoalAnimated,
             renderGoalXZBeacon, renderSelectionBoxes) from enabled to
             disabled, so pathfinding overlays never appear on a live stream.
           - Added `chatSilence` (default ENABLED): suppress Baritone's own
             chat/toast output. Baritone narrates itself ("Done building",
             "Death position saved", path failures) through logDirect, which is
             deliberately exempt from `chatDebug`, so there was no upstream way
             to quiet it. On a stream that text is on camera, and a controller
             that captures game chat can read it back as though a player had
             said it.

  MODIFIED src/api/java/baritone/api/utils/Helper.java
           logDirect(boolean, Component...) -- the single funnel every logDirect
           overload passes through -- returns early when `chatSilence` is set,
           after writing the same text to stdout so the game log keeps it for
           diagnosis. Nothing else in the tree writes to chat directly.

  MODIFIED src/main/java/baritone/process/BuilderProcess.java
           - approxPlaceable(): when getStateForPlacement returns null, fall back
             to the block's default state instead of recording AIR. That call is
             made against a synthetic BlockPlaceContext (the player's own feet,
             Direction.UP), so blocks with real placement constraints -- a chest,
             which inspects its neighbours to decide single vs double -- fail it
             routinely and were silently deleted from the placeable list.
           - Added samePlacementItem()/canSupply(), used by assemble(). One ITEM
             can place two different BLOCKS depending on the face it is clicked
             against (a torch is minecraft:torch on a floor, minecraft:wall_torch
             on a wall). Because approxPlaceable only ever reports the floor form,
             a schematic asking for a wall torch reported "missing materials" with
             a full stack of torches in the bag. This answers only "do I have the
             material"; the exact state is still chosen by the existing placement
             test, so it cannot place a floor torch where a wall torch belongs.
           - Three quit reasons also print to stdout. logDirect is in-game chat,
             which never reaches latest.log, so every reason the builder gave up
             was invisible in the logs.

  MODIFIED src/main/java/baritone/pathing/movement/CalculationContext.java
           Added the `waterForbidden` field and canCrossSmallWater(), the bounded
           crossing check backing the settings above: while dry, a movement may
           enter water only if continuing in its direction reaches shore within
           `smallWaterCrossingMaxLength` blocks. The scan is directional so a path
           cannot follow a shoreline through the water to defeat the limit. Once
           already wet the restriction lifts, so the route back to land is never
           cut off. Vanilla prices a swum block low enough that a long swim looks
           cheaper than walking around, which made the bot cross oceans.

  MODIFIED src/main/java/baritone/pathing/movement/movements/MovementTraverse.java
           src/main/java/baritone/pathing/movement/movements/MovementDiagonal.java
           Call canCrossSmallWater() before costing a water movement, on every
           branch that can enter water. Pricing alone was not enough: these are
           the movements that actually decide to swim.

  MODIFIED src/main/java/baritone/pathing/movement/movements/MovementDescend.java
           Do not break a fall by dropping into water -- that is still getting
           in the water, which the settings above exist to prevent.

  MODIFIED src/api/java/baritone/api/utils/BlockOptionalMeta.java
           The block-drop lookup swallows its exception instead of calling
           e.printStackTrace(). Fabric's loot hook wants a real MinecraftServer,
           which this lightweight client-side level deliberately does not have,
           so the throw is expected -- and one full stack trace per tracked block
           can stall the client during a rescan.

  REMOVED  dist/
           Prebuilt jars for Minecraft 1.18.0 and 1.20.4 (~12 MB) that are not
           reachable from this 26.1.2 fork. They remain available upstream.


